-- title:
--   Finitely many elements with P generating the unit ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring and let $P : S \to \mathrm{Prop}$ be an arbitrary predicate on the elements of $S$. Assume that for every point $\mathfrak p$ of the prime spectrum of $S$ whose underlying ideal is maximal there exists $g \in S$ with $g \notin \mathfrak p$ and $P(g)$. The conclusion is that there exist a natural number $k$ and a family $g : \mathrm{Fin}\,k \to S$ such that the ideal generated by the range of $g$ is the whole of $S$ and $P(g_i)$ holds for every index $i$. Thus a property that can be realised by some element outside each maximal ideal can be realised by a finite family of elements generating the unit ideal; in geometric terms, the basic open sets attached to elements satisfying $P$ cover $\operatorname{Spec} S$, and finitely many of them already do.
--
--   This is the standard finiteness device behind "spreading out" arguments: a local condition verified at every closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} S$ is upgraded to a finite distinguished cover of the whole spectrum. It is used in the construction of canonical polarisation data on fake elliptic curves, where a property established after inverting a suitable element near each maximal ideal is globalised to a faithfully flat cover given by finitely many localisations.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_exists_finset_span_eq_top_of_forall_isMaximal_exists_not_mem.lean

